\section{Block Ciphers}

A block cipher is an encryption technique that processes data in fixed-size groups, called blocks. Unlike a Caesar cipher, which shifts individual letters, block ciphers transform groups of characters together using an encryption key.\\

\noindent For this challenge, you will implement a simplified block cipher that rearranges characters within fixed-size blocks. Each block will contain four characters, and the encryption key will determine the order in which those characters appear in the encrypted message.\\

\noindent Your encryption key is:
\[
\text{Key} = [1,4,3,2]
\]

\noindent Your block cipher encoder and decoder should
\begin{itemize}
    \item Accept a plaintext message and divides it into blocks of four characters
    \item Use a one-dimensional integer array to store the encryption key
    \item Use loops and arrays to rearrange characters within each block
    \item Produce ciphertext by applying the encryption key to each block
    \item Decrypt ciphertext by reversing the encryption process
    \item Correctly handle messages whose lengths are not multiples of four
\end{itemize}

\noindent You may use a padding character of your choice to complete the final block. Make sure your program can distinguish padding from the original message when decrypting.\\

